At any given time, Marvel Studios has series planned five-to-six years out from what they have announced,<ref name="5To6YearPlan" /> with Marvel Television generally having three to five live-action series in development.<ref name="DDBAS3FinalZodiac" /> In July 2025, Feige said they had a seven-year plan through 2032 with potential projects on magnets that could be moved around. Marvel Studios was looking to release one live-action series a year at that point.<ref name="FeigeConferenceJul2025Deadline" />

By November 2022, Coogler's Proximity Media was developing several series set in [[Wakanda (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Wakanda]].<ref name="VarietyNov2022CoverStory" /> [[Giancarlo Esposito]] indicated in May 2024 that he would be featured in an MCU series after being introduced as [[Seth Voelker (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Seth Voelker / Sidewinder]] in ''[[Captain America: Brave New World]]'' (2025),<ref name="EspositoBNW" /><ref name="EspositoSeries" /> and director [[Julius Onah]] said Esposito's role in the film was set up for further appearances of Sidewinder's [[Serpent Society (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Serpent Society]].<ref name="SerpentSocietyBNW" />

== Release schedule ==
''WandaVision'' and ''The Falcon and the Winter Soldier'' were released on Fridays. The majority of Marvel Studios' series since the first season of ''Loki'' have been released on Wednesdays, although ''She-Hulk'' was released on Thursdays. The [[Loki season 2|second season]] of ''Loki'' (2023) and ''Echo'' were released in [[Prime time television in the United States|prime time in the United States]], the former on Thursday evenings and the latter on a Tuesday evening.<ref name="EchoTuesdayReleases" /> Most of Marvel Studios' series are released weekly. The [[What If...? season 2|second season]] of ''What If...?'' (2023) was the first to be released daily,<ref name="WhatIfS2Release" /> while ''Echo'' was the first to be released all at once in a [[Binge-watching|binge release]].<ref name="EchoNonWeekly" /><ref name="Sep2023TVDelays" /> ''Echo'' was also released simultaneously on [[Hulu]],<ref name="EchoTuesdayReleases" /> where it was available for a limited time,<ref name="EchoHuluAvailability" /> and was the first Marvel Studios series to be rated [[TV-MA]].<ref name="EchoTuesdayReleases" /> The [[Your Friendly Neighborhood Spider-Man season 1|first season]] of ''[[Your Friendly Neighborhood Spider-Man]]'' (2025) was released weekly in groups of two or three episodes, which Chris Agar of ''[[ComicBook.com]]'' called unusual for an MCU series. He believed this was done to prevent the season's release from overlapping with the [[Daredevil: Born Again season 1|first season]] of ''Born Again'' (2025).<ref name="YFNSMReleaseSchedule" /> After the announcement of a binge release for ''Wonder Man'', journalist Alex Zalben described Marvel's inconsistent series release strategy as "all over the place". He noted that ''Echo'' and ''Wonder Man'', the first two series under the Marvel Spotlight banner, were both "unceremoniously dumped" as binge releases in January and therefore felt the banner could indicate that Marvel Studios is not confident in a series.<ref name="WonderManReleaseSchedule" /><ref name="TotalFilmSpotlightAnalysis" /> ''[[VisionQuest]]'' (2026) released its first six episodes weekly in two-episode blocks, followed by the final two episodes releasing individually a week apart. ''[[Total Film]]''{{'s}} Bradley Russell felt this was unusual and not as "straightforward", but described it as providing a "super-sized serving" to encourage weekly discussions of [[fan theories]], similar to those for ''WandaVision'' and ''Agatha All Along''. Showrunner [[Terry Matalas]] explained that the block episodes were released as such to provide more content each week, and described the final two episodes as "their own kind of movies".<ref name="VisionQuestReleaseSchedule" />

