=== Analysis ===
Discussing the studio's October 2023 philosophy change for ''[[The A.V. Club]]'', Sam Barsanti said it was good that Marvel recognized the issues with "turn[ing] its Disney+ shows into stretched-out movies". However, Barsanti believed ''WandaVision'' and ''She-Hulk'' did not have the same problems and therefore felt it was possible to make more traditional television under the previous approach.<ref name="AVClubTVOverhaul" /> ''[[/Film]]''{{'}}s Ben Pearson called the previous approach "seriously flawed" and said "tried-and-true methodologies work for a reason"; Pearson was hopeful that other Hollywood studios would not try to replicate Marvel's initial approach.<ref name="/FilmTVOverhaul" />

Alex Zalben, writing for ''[[Total Film]]'', championed the Marvel Spotlight format following the successful release of ''Wonder Man'', the second series to use the Spotlight label after ''Echo'', believing all Marvel Studios series should use it. He argued the Spotlight label allowed Marvel Studios the ability to better explore "characters, themes, and emotions" in their series without the need to constantly set up the next project or storyline in the larger MCU, something that had become an "obsessive mentality" for the franchise, nor having them deal with "world-ending stakes" of most MCU projects. Zalben likened the Spotlight approach to how Marvel Studios created their [[Marvel Cinematic Universe: Phase One|Phase One]] films, and believed returning to this approach was the "path forward" for the MCU.<ref name="TotalFilmSpotlightAnalysis" /> ''[[Collider (website)|Collider]]''{{'s}} Carolyn Jenkins was also a fan of the Spotlight format, enjoying the character-first nature of them and how they focused less on the larger MCU narratives. As with Zalben, she believed it was a "good direction" for the MCU moving forward since the series were "less risky", "more enjoyable", and "easier to digest".<ref name="ColliderSpotlightAnalysis" /> ''[[Screen Rant]]''{{'s}} Alex Harrison said ''Wonder Man'' was unique among MCU projects because it was largely separate from other storylines, and had "made good" on what Harrison felt was "the initial promise of MCU TV, before everything needed to be as big as the movies".<ref name="CrettonSRJul2026" />

After reporting on ''Wonder Man''{{'s}} cancelation, Sneider opined that Marvel content needed to "feel special" and this had been lost when numerous series were being released each year. He felt series would no longer center on lesser-known characters such as [[Wonder Man]], and Marvel would be focusing their efforts on its animated MCU shows as well as Marvel Studios Animation's [[Marvel Studios Animation#Disney Jr. series and specials|pre-school series]].<ref name="SneiderJul2026" /> ''[[ComicBook.com]]''{{'s}} Chris Agar similarly thought that green-lighting multiple new live-action series would contradict Feige's comments about wanting the MCU to be easier to follow after the film ''[[Avengers: Secret Wars]]'' (2027), and that Disney+ likely "ultimately did more harm than good" by over-saturating audiences and leading to "feelings of franchise fatigue".<ref name="AgarAug2026" /> After Marvel Studios announced several animated series continuations (such as the ''Marvel Zombies'' specials), the live-action spy thriller series ''Zodiac'' (2028), and that ''Daredevil: Born Again'' would conclude with its third season, Ray Flook at ''[[Bleeding Cool]]'' opined that the live-action series were being made the "scapegoat" for the "bigger-picture issues" with the MCU series output.<ref name="FlookOct2026" />

In November 2026, Casey Loving at ''[[TheWrap]]'' discussed the first era of Marvel Television series, from ''WandaVision'' through the third season of ''Daredevil: Born Again'', in conversation with Dave Gonzales, co-author of the book ''[[MCU: The Reign of Marvel Studios]]'' (2023), and Jon Giegengack, founder of Hub Entertainment Research. Loving said the era had "some pretty sharp highs and lows" and was filled with inconsistency, from varied viewership, "infrequent renewals, [and] repeated backtracking". He said too many of the series relied heavily on connecting to the MCU films, which in turn set up unrealistic expectations for characters to appear further, such as Moon Knight. When characters introduced in series did appear in films, it resulted in "weaker-than-normal box office hauls" because audiences felt they had to watch all of the series to understand the film. Gonzales said Marvel Television had been "halfway successful" in intertwining the series with the films, but they did not have the same post-production flexibility that the films have to connect projects with the next release. Gonzales believed Marvel did not embrace the television development model "until far too late in the process". Moving forward, Loving, Gonzales, and Giegengack hoped Marvel Television would continue to embrace the showrunner development model, finding people who have a passion for a certain character; continue to experiment with genres; use more modest budgeting to plan for multi-season series rather than creating miniseries that deter audience investment; try to entice new viewers with series designed to be accessible within the franchise, such as embracing the original intent of the "Spotlight" banner and only loosely referencing other MCU events; and release less content overall to make each release feel special. Giegengack felt a weekly release strategy for each series would be better than a binge model because it allows the most conversations and engagement for the series as possible.<ref name="WrapNov2026TV5Things" />

== Repurposed, abandoned, or paused projects ==
The following projects were in development as television series before being redeveloped as potential films:

* '''''Armor Wars''''': In December 2020, Marvel Studios announced ''Armor Wars'' as a Disney+ series based on the comic book storyline "[[Armor Wars]]" (1987–1988), with [[Don Cheadle]] reprising his role as [[James Rhodes (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|James Rhodes / War Machine]].<ref name="Dec2020Series" /> In August 2021, [[Yassir Lester]] was hired as [[head writer]].<ref name="LesterAW" /> In September 2022, Marvel Studios decided to rework the series into a feature film, with Cheadle and Lester remaining with the project.<ref name="AWChangeToFilm" /> Development slowed down by February 2025.<ref name="AWMooreFeb2025" />
* '''Nova''': In March 2022, writer [[Sabir Pirzada]] was revealed to be developing a [[Nova (Richard Rider)|Richard Rider / Nova]] series.<ref name="NovaProjectPirzada" /><ref name="NovaFeigeJul2024" /> Development slowed down by February 2023.<ref name="THROutputRethinkFeb2023" /> In December 2024, [[Ed Bernero]] replaced Pirzada as writer and showrunner,<ref name="BerneroNova" /> before the series was put on pause in February 2025.<ref name="DevelopmentPauseFeb2025" /> In July 2026, [[Michael Waldron]] was reported to be working on a script for a Nova feature film.<ref name="WaldronNovaFilm" />

The following projects were abandoned or canceled by Marvel Television during development:

* '''Okoye series''': In February 2021, a drama series set in Wakanda was revealed to be in development from Ryan Coogler and Proximity Media.<ref name="CooglerSeries" /><ref name="WakandaSeriesMarvel" /> [[Danai Gurira]] signed on to reprise her film role as [[Okoye (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Okoye]], the head of the [[Dora Milaje (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Dora Milaje]], by that May. It was said to be an [[origin story]] spin-off for the character.<ref name="GuriraOriginSeries" /><ref name="GuriraCollider" /> In November 2022, Marvel Studios executive [[Nate Moore (film producer)|Nate Moore]] said the series was not "far along" due to their focus on ''Wakanda Forever'' and its spin-off series ''Ironheart''.<ref name="MooreNov2022" /> Gurira confirmed in January 2023 that there had been discussions surrounding an Okoye-led series.<ref name="GuriraJan2023" /> In April, Moore said the studio was wary about detracting from the "cinematic experience" of the franchise by featuring characters from the ''Black Panther'' films in Disney+ series.<ref name="MooreApr2023" /> In February 2025, Winderbaum said the series was no longer being made.<ref name="WinderbaumOkoyeFeb2025" />
* '''''Wonder Man'' season 2''': In July 2026, a second season of ''Wonder Man'' was canceled after it had been announced that March.<ref name="WonderManS2Canceled" /><ref name="WonderManS2Renewal" /> Co-creators [[Andrew Guest]] and [[Destin Daniel Cretton]] had planned to return as showrunner and director, respectively, along with stars [[Yahya Abdul-Mateen II]] and [[Ben Kingsley]].<ref name="WonderManS2Renewal" /> Guest had written the first episode along with an outline for the season, with the [[writers' room]] scheduled to begin in August 2026 for a planned filming start in early 2027. He said the series was not shifting to be a feature film.<ref name="GuestWMCancellation" /> The planned writers were released to pursue other projects.<ref name="WonderManS2Canceled" />

The following projects were in development but were paused in February 2025. There was potential for them to come to fruition at some point, but Marvel Studios was prioritizing other projects at that time:<ref name="DevelopmentPauseFeb2025" /> 

* '''''Terror Inc.''''': An adaptation of the comic book ''[[Terror Inc.]]'' (1988–1992).<ref name="DevelopmentPauseFeb2025" />
* '''''Strange Academy''''': An adaptation of the comic book ''[[Strange Academy]]'' (2020–2022). [[Amy Rardin]] was developing the series, which was expected to focus on [[Benedict Wong]]'s MCU character [[Wong (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Wong]] as he leads a school for young people with magical abilities.<ref name="DevelopmentPauseFeb2025" />

